Z||000003||tgowens||12-13-2001||03:42 PM||||Lisa, if we published your name in our magazine we would spell it L I S A - right?<P>Roy, congratulations on the article. You deserve the recognition!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg">  <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||CoolBlue87GT||12-13-2001||03:53 PM||||Great ! <P>A friend called me yesterday, told me my car is in there also.<P>Have you seen it ?<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/CoolBluesig.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000005||mrfixit58||12-13-2001||03:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR><B>What was the article about...?  Hey- why not just post it here for the rest of us to read   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  (Those of us who are either not FOCOA members, or those of use who are- and just aren't recieving the magazine...)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Well, you asked for it:  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>Restoration Tip<P>Two weeks before the big show in Daytona, my daughter pulled one of those “think fast!” drills with me. Well, time must be catching up with me because, obviously, I DIDN’T think fast enough and the football she was throwing sailed right passed me and, as luck would have it, hit and broke my right taillight lens on my 87 GT. What happened next, I’d rather not say but suffice it to say there will be no more projectiles thrown toward Daddy’s cars again.<P>Fortunately, I had an extra set of GT taillights stored in the loft of the garage. Unfortunately, the reason they were the SPARE set is because they looked like someone had laid them face down and slid them across a broom finished concrete floor. We’re talking MAJOR scratches. Since scratched taillights we better then broken taillights, I decided to try and clean them up.<P>I started by first wet sanding then with 400 grit to remove the deeper scratches. I followed this with 600, 800, and 1000 grit. Next I tried to use regular buffing compound to return the original luster but that didn’t work very well. That’s when I decided to take a trip to the local NAPA Auto Parts store and see what they had to offer. What I found was 3M’s Plastic lens Cleaner (Part # 39017) and Plastic Lens Polish (Part # 39010).<P>It took three applications of the plastic cleaner but after each one, the scratches faded and the lens became clearer. I finished up with one application of the polish to really make them shine. The scratches just disappeared and the like-new luster returned. I then tried the cleaner on the triangular filler pieces behind the side view mirrors and along the black molding. Each time, the cleaner removed all the spotting and returned a like-new finish. For those of you who may have tried this stuff, you’d have to agree that it is truly an amazing product. Now, if 3M just made something for my reflexes…<P>Roy Lumsden <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000006||Hartz||12-13-2001||03:58 PM||||The editing in their newsletters is atrocious.  I'm not great at that kind of stuff, myself, but if I was sending out a national publication I'd at least proof read it!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P><BR>Hey, maybe this belongs in the other thread about FOCOA!     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Hartz<BR>||||reg||1
